<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Mainland Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Suphannaphum</h1><p class="st-meta">1150 CE – 1438 CE (approx.) · capital Suphanburi</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Thai kingdom west of the Chao Phraya, centred on Suphanburi, whose ruling house took over Ayutthaya and swallowed Sukhothai.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Suphannaphum emerged as a Tai polity around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suphanburi">Suphanburi</a> in the 12th century, in an area earlier settled by <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/dvaravati">Dvaravati</a> communities. Chinese records of missions from Xian, often taken to mean this region, suggest a growing Thai power in the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chao-phraya-river">Chao Phraya</a> by the late 13th century.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The kingdom was based on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> farming and trade along the western rivers. Its royal house, sometimes called the Suphannaphum dynasty, intermarried with Lavo. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramathibodi-i">U Thong</a> founded <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya</a> in 1351, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borommarachathirat-i">Khun Luang Pa-ngua of Suphanburi</a>, his brother-in-law, seized the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya</a> throne in 1370 as Borommarachathirat I and campaigned against <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/sukhothai-kingdom">Sukhothai</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Suphannaphum effectively merged with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya</a>, whose kings from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/intharacha">Intharacha</a> onward were of its line. In 1438 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borommarachathirat-ii">Borommarachathirat II</a> absorbed the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/sukhothai-kingdom">Sukhothai Kingdom</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suphanburi">Suphanburi</a> became a provincial city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya</a>. Its rulers had thus shaped the core of the later Siamese state.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/suphannaphum.jpg" alt="Suphannaphum" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Nawa1509 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E0%B9%83%E0%B8%95%E0%B9%89%E0%B8%A3%E0%B9%88%E0%B8%A1%E0%B9%82%E0%B8%9E%E0%B8%98%E0%B8%B4%E0%B9%8C.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1150 CE – 1438 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Suphanburi</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Borommarachathirat I, Intharacha</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Theravada Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Thai</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/thailand">Thailand</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Suphannabhumi, Suphanburi kingdom, Suvarnabhumi</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1294&amp;focus=suphannaphum" aria-label="Suphannaphum on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7428 1601 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Suphannaphum lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7752 1900h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7748 1857h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M7748 1857h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Thailand. <a href="/atlas?year=1294&amp;focus=suphannaphum">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
